WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_121'), 'A first version of the validator stops at the first problem it finds. Trace for one ticket:

turn 4  model -> list_submissions {"challenge":"chal_ts_042","limit":"10","status":"passed"}
        tool  -> error: unexpected argument "challenge"
turn 5  model -> list_submissions {"challenge_id":"chal_ts_042","limit":"10","status":"passed"}
        tool  -> error: "limit" must be integer
turn 6  model -> list_submissions {"challenge_id":"chal_ts_042","limit":10,"status":"passed"}
        tool  -> error: "status" must be one of "accepted", "rejected", "pending"
turn 7  model -> list_submissions {"challenge_id":"chal_ts_042","limit":10,"status":"accepted"}
        tool  -> ok (3 rows)

input tokens per model call: turn 4: 6,900  turn 5: 7,300  turn 6: 7,650  turn 7: 8,020

Statement: "Stopping at the first violation is the cheaper design here: each error message is shorter, so these extra retries cost about the same as one retry that lists all three problems."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The cost of a retry is not the length of the error message, it is the model call: every turn resends the whole transcript, here about 7,000 input tokens and a few seconds each. Turns 5 and 6 exist only because the model learned about one problem at a time. A validator that lists every violation (missing challenge_id, unexpected challenge, limit must be integer, status must be one of ...) lets the model fix all of them in turn 5, saving two full calls, roughly 15,000 input tokens and their latency. The misconception is to optimise the size of the error instead of the number of turns.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
