WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_131'), 'Part of the loop after a first fix:

for (const call of calls) {
    try {
        results.push({ role: "tool", callId: call.id, name: call.name, ok: true, result: await tools.get(call.name)!.run(call.args) });
    } catch (err) {
        return { status: "failed", answer: "We couldn''t process your request. A human will get back to you." };
    }
}

Trace of ticket #9131 with this loop:

turn 1  model -> call_1 list_orders {}
        tool  -> threw TimeoutError: orders API timed out after 5000 ms
        run   -> failed, fallback reply

The same ticket replayed with a scripted model, the error appended as { ok: false, error: { code: "tool_failed", message: "orders API timed out after 5000 ms" } }:

turn 2  model -> call_2 list_orders {}
        tool  -> ok [ord_88 paid, ord_89 authorization_pending]

Statement: "Ending the run when a tool throws is the safer design here, because the model has no way to use the result of a failed tool call."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A failed tool call is information the model can act on: retry once (as in the replay, where the second list_orders succeeded), use another tool, ask the learner, or say it could not check and hand over. Feeding the failure back as a structured entry (code tool_failed and a short message) keeps the run going and leaves the decision to the model, while the loop still controls how often it may try (step limits, lesson 3.1). Ending the run turns every transient timeout into a failed ticket, which is what filled the human queue in the incident. Errors the model cannot fix, such as the model API rejecting the request or a bug in the loop, are the ones that should stop the run. The misconception is that stopping is always the safe default; for a read-only lookup it only makes the agent fail more often.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_131') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
