SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', false),
        ('False', true)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);
WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_131'), 'Turn 3 of a run, and what the loop appended:

turn 3  model -> [call_3 get_order {"order_id":"ord_88"}, call_4 get_progress {"course_slug":42}]

transcript after turn 3:
  { role: "assistant", toolCalls: [call_3 get_order, call_4 get_progress] }
  { role: "tool", callId: "call_3", name: "get_order", ok: true, result: { id: "ord_88", status: "paid" } }

loop code for each call:
  const violations = tool.validate(call.args);
  if (violations.length > 0) continue;          // skip invalid calls

next model request -> 400 invalid_request: tool call call_4 has no matching tool result

Statement: "The request fails because call_4 was skipped when it failed validation; appending an invalid_arguments error for call_4 after call_3''s result lets the run continue and tells the model what to fix."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The model API (and the fake model in the exercise) requires one result for every tool call of the previous turn, in order. The loop recorded call_4 in the assistant message but appended nothing for it, so the next request is rejected before the model ever sees it. Appending { role: "tool", callId: "call_4", ok: false, error: { code: "invalid_arguments", message: "\"course_slug\" must be string" } } after call_3''s result satisfies the protocol and tells the model exactly which argument to fix. The misconception is that an invalid call can simply be ignored; every call the model proposed needs an answer, even if the answer is an error.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_131') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO challenge.question_options (question_id, option_text, is_correct)
SELECT q.id, o.option_text, o.is_correct
FROM q, (VALUES
        ('True', true),
        ('False', false)
    ) AS o(option_text, is_correct);

-- challenge chal_coding_aam_132 (mini_coding)
INSERT INTO challenge.challenges (public_id, course_id, module_id, lesson_id, name, title, description, status, difficulty, type, points, is_published, is_deleted)
VALUES ('chal_coding_aam_132', (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'ai-agents-mcp'), (SELECT id FROM course.module WHERE course_id = (SELECT id FROM catalog.course WHERE public_id = 'ai-agents-mcp') AND order_index = 1 AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), (SELECT id FROM course.lesson WHERE content_source = 'lesson-aam-1-3' AND is_deleted = false LIMIT 1), 'agent-loop-fake-model', 'The agent loop, run against a scripted fake model', 'The CodeDev support agent runs one loop: ask the model for a turn, run the tool calls it proposes, append the results, repeat. Incident: the shipped loop ran only the first of two parallel calls (the model API then rejected the request: tool call call_4 has no matching tool result), let a tool timeout escape as an exception, and crashed on unknown tool names; 212 of 1,980 runs in one week ended in the fallback reply. Write runAgent(model, tools, task) with tools: Map<string, Tool>. Start the transcript with { role: "user", content: task } and repeat: (1) turn = model.next(transcript); do not catch errors thrown by model.next, let them propagate. (2) A turn { final } appends { role: "assistant", final } and returns { answer: final, transcript, stats }. (3) A turn { toolCall } is one call, { toolCalls } several calls proposed together. Give each call the id call_<n>, n counting from 1 over the whole run in the order proposed, append ONE message { role: "assistant", toolCalls: [{ id, name, args }, ...] }, then handle the calls in that order and append one tool message per call in the same order. A tool message is { role: "tool", callId, name, ok: true, result } or { role: "tool", callId, name, ok: false, error: { code, message } }: no tool with that name in the map: code unknown_tool, message unknown tool "<name>"; available tools: <tool names in map order, joined by ", ">. tool.validate(args) returns violations: code invalid_arguments, message the violations joined by "; ", and the tool does not run. tool.run(args) throws: code tool_failed, message the Error''s message, a thrown string as is, any other value JSON.stringify''d. Otherwise ok: true with the returned value as result. A failing call never stops the run or the other calls of its turn. (4) Ask the model again. There is no step limit in this exercise; the fake model throws when asked for more than 50 turns. stats counts modelTurns (turns returned by model.next), toolCalls (calls proposed), executed (calls to tool.run, including the ones that threw), invalidArguments, unknownTools and toolFailures. The fake model in the runner returns its scripted turns in order (repeating the last one) and, like a real model API, throws if the transcript does not start with the task, does not hold one assistant message per earlier turn with the calls it proposed, reuses a call id, or does not answer every call of its previous turn in order. Input (stdin): { "task", "tools": [{ name, parameters, outcomes }], "script": ModelTurn[], "summary"?: true }. The runner prints the transcript (with summary: its length and a checksum), the answer, the stats and how often each tool ran, or error: <message> when runAgent throws. Do not change the types or the test runner.', 'open', 'hard'::difficulty_question, 'mini_coding', 40, true, false)
