WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_311'), 'Limits: maxSteps 20, maxSameCall 3 (stop when the same call is proposed 3 times in a row), maxNoProgress 3 (stop after 3 tool results in a row that were already seen in this run).

Trace of run support-8812 ("Which of my orders can I still refund?"):
step 1  find_orders {"email":"lan@example.com"}  -> orders of lan@example.com: o-1001, o-1003
step 2  get_order {"orderId":"o-1001"}          -> order o-1001: python-data, paid
step 3  get_order {"orderId":"o-1003"}          -> order o-1003: sql-basics, paid
step 4  get_order {"orderId":"o-1001"}          -> order o-1001: python-data, paid
step 5  get_order {"orderId":"o-1003"}          -> order o-1003: sql-basics, paid
step 6  get_order {"orderId":"o-1001"}          -> order o-1001: python-data, paid
...

Statement: "In this run the repeated-call check never fires; the run is stopped by the no-progress check, at step 6."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The repeated-call check counts identical proposals in a row, and here no call follows itself: the model alternates between o-1001 and o-1003, so the count never goes past 1. The no-progress check looks at results instead: steps 1-3 each bring a new text, step 4 repeats step 2''s result (1), step 5 repeats step 3''s (2) and step 6 repeats step 2''s again (3), which reaches maxNoProgress at step 6. The misconception is that a repeated-call check catches every loop; a model that cycles between two or more calls slips past it, which is why it is paired with a progress check and a hard step limit.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
