    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_431'), 'Proposed fix (diff of agent/loop.ts and agent/gateway.ts):
+ if (tool.permission === "write") {
+   const answer = desk.request(session.id, call);     // shown to a support lead, who approves or declines this exact call
+   if ("declined" in answer) { transcript.push({ role: "tool", content: answer.declined }); continue; }
+   token = answer.token;                                // single-use, bound to session + tool + canonical args
+ }
  // gateway: a write runs only if desk.redeem(token, session.id, call) succeeds
- transcript.push({ role: "tool", content: output });
+ transcript.push({ role: "tool", content: wrapToolOutput(call.name, trust, output) });

Only the approval desk''s UI, used by people, can create tokens.

Statement: "Even if the model still proposed all 12 refunds after this fix, none of them would run unless a support lead approved that exact refund."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The wrapper makes the injection less likely to work, but the guarantee comes from the approval path: each write is shown to a person, and the gateway only runs a write whose token was issued for that session, tool and canonical arguments and has not been used. The model cannot produce a token, and a token for one refund does not fit another. That is why the fix still holds if the filter misses a cleverly worded instruction. The trade-off to watch is approval fatigue: if every write needs a click, narrow the tools and add limits so people review few, meaningful calls.', 5, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_431') AND order_index = 5)
