WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_511'), 'Workflow "Refund triage", executions on 2026-10-23 (UTC+7):
17:02:11  Success  ticket #48213
17:05:40  Success  ticket #48217

Refund API access log for the same calls (UTC):
2026-10-23T10:02:12Z POST /admin/refunds 503 caller=token:codedev-admin ticket=48213
2026-10-23T10:05:41Z POST /admin/refunds 503 caller=token:codedev-admin ticket=48217

Workflow export (simplified):
{ "name": "Issue refund", "type": "http", "method": "POST", "onError": "continue" }

Statement: "Because the workflow tool marked both runs as Success, the refunds for tickets 48213 and 48217 were created."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The API answered 503 to both calls, so no refund was created. The run is marked Success because the HTTP step is set to continue on error: the failure is swallowed, the workflow moves on, and the next step can tell the learner the refund was issued. An execution status describes what the workflow engine did with the steps, not whether the system on the other side did the work. The misconception is that the orchestration tool''s status is the source of truth for a write; check the API''s own log or audit rows for the same caller, and set write steps to stop the run and alert on error.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_511') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
