WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_521'), 'The same 50 support scenarios, real model, run on two designs:

                         A: one agent + new tool get_my_order_charges   B: orchestrator + 4 workers + critic
scenarios passed         47 / 50                                        45 / 50
tokens, median           8.1k                                           52k
tokens, p95              13k                                            96k
latency, p95             8 s                                            38 s

get_my_order_charges takes an orderId, checks the order belongs to the session user and returns the captured charges with duplicateCharge computed in code.

Statement: "On this evidence, design A is the better choice for the support assistant."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Design A passes more scenarios with about a sixth of the median tokens and less than a quarter of the p95 latency. The multi-agent design adds cost, latency and more places for errors to enter, and on this task it buys nothing measurable; the gain came from a better tool, which moved the charge matching into code and added an ownership check. The misconception is that splitting work across specialised agents makes a system more accurate by default. Multi-agent designs earn their cost on independent subtasks that each need a large context, which a two-lookup support question is not.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_521') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
