+ Be proactive: if a learner might need follow-up, open a ticket so nobody is forgotten.

CI: test/agentLoop.test.ts  48 passed, 0 failed

A typical test in that suite:
const model = fakeModel([
  { toolCall: { name: "get_enrollment", args: { courseId: "rust-practical" } } },
  { final: "Your access is active until 2027-01-31." },
]);
const run = await runAgent({ model, tools, policy, budget, userMessage: "When does my Rust course access end?" });
assert.deepEqual(run.toolCalls.map((c) => c.name), ["get_enrollment"]);

Statement: "The 48 passing tests show that prompt v15 does not make the agent open tickets it does not need."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The fake model returns the same scripted turns whatever the system prompt says, so the suite produces the same result for v14 and v15; it cannot see a behaviour change caused by the prompt. Those tests prove the harness: the loop, limits, permissions and audit. A prompt, model or tool-description change needs a scenario eval with the real model, for example an access-end-date scenario with open_ticket forbidden, run several times. The misconception is that a green CI run covers every kind of change; it only covers the code paths the fakes exercise.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_aam_531') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
